	<div id="header">
		<h1 id="site_title"><a href="http://starfish.dreamhosters.com">Starfish</a></h1>
		<p id="site_tagline">Find the best deals and everything you want to know about Starfish.</p>
	</div>
	<div class="columns-3">
		<div class="c1">
			<div id="post-1740" class="post_box top type-page status-publish">
				<div class="headline_area">
					<h1 class="headline">Starfish</h1>
				</div>
				<div class="post_content">
<p>The Starfish are by far the most diverse group of the echinoderms that can be observed on the shore, deviating on many occasions from the basic five-part segment plan, and having a wide range of colours. Even stars of the same species can differ greatly in colouration and number of arms.</p>
<p>Like the urchins, the mouth is located centrally on the underside of the main body. The tube feet run on a single deep groove along each arm from tip to mouth. These feet are operated by a complicated water system in the same fashion as the urchins; the water being drawn into a small sieve plate which is located on the upper surface.</p>
<p>The majority of Starfish are carnivores, feeding mainly on shellfish. Working in concert, the tube feet can exert extreme tension and are able to open a bivalve merely by prolonging this tension for as long as is necessary on both halves of the shell. If the prey is too large to be drawn into the mouth, starfish can usually spit out their entire stomach, allowing the digestive juices to operate outside the body and withdrawing once more when the meal is finished.</p>
<p>Stars are often found with several arms in various stages of regeneration, the loss usually due to accident or predation. This ability to replace missing parts must give the starfishes longer lives in might otherwise be expected, and larger species may live for over 40 years.</p>
<p>Some Starfish developed very young in brood pouch held in place by the spines, or clustered around the parents mouth. Other species can sprout a juvenile directly from their own tissue. Eventually the youngster breaks free and moves off, leaving the parent to regenerate the small amount of missing tissue.</p>
<p>Starfish are easy to see from the beach or rocks, however, to view the more magnificent specimens it is necessary to go either snorkeling or scuba diving. To get to the best diving spots I recommend going on one of the Southern Caribbean Cruises. These will take you to some of the most beautiful areas in the world.</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
